<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amannisa Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1526–1560</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amannisa Khan (1526–1560) was a poet, musician and queen of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkent-khanate">Yarkent Khanate</a> as the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rashid-khan">Abd al-Rashid Khan</a>. She is credited with collecting and arranging the classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghur-muqam">Uyghur Muqam</a>, the great cycle of songs, dances and instrumental pieces, and with writing poetry and works on music. She is honoured as a central figure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> culture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amannisa-khan.jpg" alt="Amannisa Khan" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Hill · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Amanni_Shahan._Yarkand._2011.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
